dit":null,"pinnedBy":null,"body":"\u003e \u003e Why is this a tag, and not a property on the `\u003cselectlist\u003e` tag? It could simply be the `value` property, matching the pattern of other form elements.\r\n\u003e \r\n\u003e I'm not sure I understand what the API shape would look like. Here is an example of how selectedoption would be used:\r\n\r\n[snip]\r\n\r\n\u003e How would you rewrite this without the selectedoption tag?\r\n\r\nI guess my initial thought was that the form would simply submit text, in which case this would be sufficient:\r\n```\r\n\u003cselectlist value=\"one\"\u003e\r\n  \u003cbutton type=selectlist\u003e\r\n    \u003cspan\u003eselected option:\u003c/span\u003e\r\n  \u003c/button\u003e\r\n  \u003clistbox\u003e\r\n    \u003coption\u003eone\u003c/option\u003e\r\n    \u003coption\u003etwo\u003c/option\u003e\r\n  \u003c/listbox\u003e\r\n\u003c/selectlist\u003e\r\n```\r\n\r\nOverall, I'm finding this whole structure overly verbose, but I'm beginning to see how it can be useful for styling.  \r\n\r\nThe problem I'm having with a whole element/tag for the selected option, at least the way I'm seeing it here, is that it goes against the DRY principle.  This has consequences in regards to redundancy (duplication of data, duplication of effort for programming at all levels), data consistency/integrity (the selected option, if not null, should match exactly the item in the list it's aiming to represent, but an author could mess this up).\r\n\r\nPerhaps a better alternative is to use a reference instead of duplicating the data.\r\n```\r\n\u003cselectlist\u003e\r\n  \u003cbutton type=selectlist\u003e\r\n    \u003cspan\u003eselected option:\u003c/span\u003e\r\n    \u003cselectedoption ref=\"op1\"/\u003e\r\n  \u003c/button\u003e\r\n  \u003clistbox\u003e\r\n    \u003coption key=\"op1\"\u003e\u003ci\u003eone\u003c/i\u003e\u003c/option\u003e\r\n    \u003coption key=\"op2\"\u003e\u003cb\u003etwo\u003c/b\u003e\u003c/option\u003e\r\n  \u003c/listbox\u003e\r\n\u003c/selectlist\u003e\r\n```\r\nThe browser could then fill in the whole content of the option for 'op1' in the UI, and send that same data to the server when submitting.  When the user changes the selection to 'op2', the browser then simply updates the `ref` value.\r\n\r\nI'm not really attached to the `ref` and `key` names for the properties, it's just what popped into mind.  The point is to have a way to identify different options without having to textually replicate the whole content.  I don't recommend relying on `id` because that's required to be unique thought out the whole document, whereas `key` would just need to be unique within the `selectlist` context.\r\n","bodyHTML":"\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eWhy is this a tag, and not a property on the \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u0026lt;selectlist\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003e tag? It could simply be the \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003evalue\u003c/code\u003e property, matching the pattern of other form elements.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eI'm not sure I understand what the API shape would look like. Here is an example of how selectedoption would be used:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003e[snip]\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eHow would you rewrite this without the selectedoption tag?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eI guess my initial thought was that the form would simply submit text, in which case this would be sufficient:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"snippet-clipboard-content notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"\u0026lt;selectlist value=\u0026quot;one\u0026quot;\u0026gt;\n  \u0026lt;button type=selectlist\u0026gt;\n    \u0026lt;span\u0026gt;selected option:\u0026lt;/span\u0026gt;\n  \u0026lt;/button\u0026gt;\n  \u0026lt;listbox\u0026gt;\n    \u0026lt;option\u0026gt;one\u0026lt;/option\u0026gt;\n    \u0026lt;option\u0026gt;two\u0026lt;/option\u0026gt;\n  \u0026lt;/listbox\u0026gt;\n\u0026lt;/selectlist\u0026gt;\"\u003e\u003cpre class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u0026lt;selectlist value=\"one\"\u0026gt;\n  \u0026lt;button type=selectlist\u0026gt;\n    \u0026lt;span\u0026gt;selected option:\u0026lt;/span\u0026gt;\n  \u0026lt;/button\u0026gt;\n  \u0026lt;listbox\u0026gt;\n    \u0026lt;option\u0026gt;one\u0026lt;/option\u0026gt;\n    \u0026lt;option\u0026gt;two\u0026lt;/option\u0026gt;\n  \u0026lt;/listbox\u0026gt;\n\u0026lt;/selectlist\u0026gt;\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eOverall, I'm finding this whole structure overly verbose, but I'm beginning to see how it can be useful for styling.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eThe problem I'm having with a whole element/tag for the selected option, at least the way I'm seeing it here, is that it goes against the DRY principle.  This has consequences in regards to redundancy (duplication of data, duplication of effort for programming at all levels), data consistency/integrity (the selected option, if not null, should match exactly the item in the list it's aiming to represent, but an author could mess this up).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003ePerhaps a better alternative is to use a reference instead of duplicating the data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"snippet-clipboard-content notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"\u0026lt;selectlist\u0026gt;\n  \u0026lt;button type=selectlist\u0026gt;\n    \u0026lt;span\u0026gt;selected option:\u0026lt;/span\u0026gt;\n    \u0026lt;selectedoption ref=\u0026quot;op1\u0026quot;/\u0026gt;\n  \u0026lt;/button\u0026gt;\n  \u0026lt;listbox\u0026gt;\n    \u0026lt;option key=\u0026quot;op1\u0026quot;\u0026gt;\u0026lt;i\u0026gt;one\u0026lt;/i\u0026gt;\u0026lt;/option\u0026gt;\n    \u0026lt;option key=\u0026quot;op2\u0026quot;\u0026gt;\u0026lt;b\u0026gt;two\u0026lt;/b\u0026gt;\u0026lt;/option\u0026gt;\n  \u0026lt;/listbox\u0026gt;\n\u0026lt;/selectlist\u0026gt;\"\u003e\u003cpre class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003e\u0026lt;selectlist\u0026gt;\n  \u0026lt;button type=selectlist\u0026gt;\n    \u0026lt;span\u0026gt;selected option:\u0026lt;/span\u0026gt;\n    \u0026lt;selectedoption ref=\"op1\"/\u0026gt;\n  \u0026lt;/button\u0026gt;\n  \u0026lt;listbox\u0026gt;\n    \u0026lt;option key=\"op1\"\u0026gt;\u0026lt;i\u0026gt;one\u0026lt;/i\u0026gt;\u0026lt;/option\u0026gt;\n    \u0026lt;option key=\"op2\"\u0026gt;\u0026lt;b\u0026gt;two\u0026lt;/b\u0026gt;\u0026lt;/option\u0026gt;\n  \u0026lt;/listbox\u0026gt;\n\u0026lt;/selectlist\u0026gt;\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eThe browser could then fill in the whole content of the option for 'op1' in the UI, and send that same data to the server when submitting.  When the user changes the selection to 'op2', the browser then simply updates the \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003eref\u003c/code\u003e value.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp dir=\"auto\"\u003eI'm not really attached to the \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003eref\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003ekey\u003c/code\u003e names for the properties, it's just what popped into mind.  The point is to have a way to identify different options without having to textually replicate the whole content.  I don't recommend relying on \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003eid\u003c/code\u003e because that's required to be unique thought out the whole document, whereas \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003ekey\u003c/code\u003e would just need to be unique within the \u003ccode class=\"notranslate\"\u003eselectlist\u003c/code\u003e context.\u003c/p\u003e","bodyVersion":"08086eb724b4b8cf67b8256c1ffe17c941199b598b8baceac5f6a73518430869","isHidden":false,"viewerCanUpdate":false,"repository":{"id":"MDEwOlJlcG9za